00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:22.460
Holding cash in a TFSA looks safe on paper, but quietly loses purchasing power when inflation

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:24.620
outpaces the low yields on savings.

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:30.020
settings. Today's environment of sticky inflation and shifting rate expectations makes this

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:35.820
risk concrete for Canadian investors who default to cash after market volatility. The

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:41.060
approach is to run a simple inflation-adjusted return screen on any cash allocation above

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:47.900
20% of the TFSA, comparing it against short-term bond ETFs or dividend payers with growing

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:53.980
payouts. Platforms like wealth simple or quest trade let you set automatic sweeps from cash

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:59.720
into a low-cost bond ladder, or covered call ETF, once the real yield turns negative.

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:09.660
This strategy performed best in the 2022-2023 period, when cash returns lagged CPI by more than 4 percentage points annually.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:18.580
The main risk is opportunity cost if rates rise faster than expected, so cap the shift at 10% of the TFSA per quarter.

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:24.600
Investors can start by exporting their current cash balance from the brokerage dashboard and

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:31.140
subtracting the most recent 12-month CPI figure to arrive at a real yield number. If that figure

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:37.320
sits below zero, the next step is selecting a short duration bond ETF with a management fee

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:44.580
under 0.20% and placing a limit order during the first hour of trading. The same screen can be

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:49.600
rerun quarterly to keep the allocation aligned with changing rate paths from both the Bank

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:58.080
of Canada and the Federal Reserve. Imagine you opened a TFSA in January 2023 with $6,500

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:05.140
in unused room and contributed the full amount on February 1. Your order filled, but the actual

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:11.920
room calculation resets every January 1. Based on the prior year's unused balance, plus the new

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:18.280
annual limit, not on the date you clicked by. Most retail investors do not realize that over

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:24.820
contributing by even $100 triggers a 1% per month penalty on the excess until corrected.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:30.140
The pro tip is to log into your CRA my account before each contribution

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:35.620
and export the exact available room figure rather than relying on your broker's estimate.

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:42.620
The common misconception is treating the TFSA like an RRSP, where contribution room is

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:44.120
lost forever if unused.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:50.300
The fix is to treat every January first as a fresh planning checkpoint and set a calendar

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:53.480
reminder to check the CRA balance before moving money.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:59.980
The CRA system also carries forward unused room from earlier years without limit, which means

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:06.540
a contributor who skipped 2024 can still use that room in 2026 alongside the new annual

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:11.680
allotment. Checking the official figure prevents the common error of double counting room

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:16.340
that a brokerage app has already estimated incorrectly after a recent withdrawal.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:21.460
Setting the reminder for the first business day of each year creates a repeatable habit

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:27.320
that keeps contribution decisions aligned with the actual CRA ledger rather than an approximation.

